# Typed Signals In Actions And Flow

This example calculates a rectangle area, then uses the completion to build a
label request. Each Action accepts and returns a complete native Signal. The
second request is created from the first result, after that result is available.
No provider, credentials, Bus, global registry, or messaging runtime is needed.

Run the cells from top to bottom. This Jido integration guide uses Action and
Signal together. Action accepts custom Zoi validators and remains independent
of Signal.

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:jido_action, "~> 3.0.0-beta.20"},
    {:jido_signal, "~> 3.0.0-beta.5"}
  ],
  start_applications: false
)

{:ok, _apps} = Application.ensure_all_started(:jido_action)
```

This guide uses the public `Jido.Signal.Catalog` API. Signal does not provide
a complete, definition-specific Zoi schema helper. That work
is tracked in [Signal #222](https://github.com/agentjido/jido_signal/issues/222).
The [schema export section](#schema-export-limit) states the resulting limit.
The example does not define a replacement Signal schema API.

## Define The Signal Family

Use one explicit definition for each request, completion, and domain failure.
String data keys also work with JSON wire data. Each definition fixes both its
type and its schema identity.

```elixir
defmodule SignalExamples.AreaRequested do
  use Jido.Signal,
    type: "example.area.requested",
    dataschema: "urn:example:area:requested:1",
    schema:
      Zoi.object(%{
        "width" => Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.min(1),
        "height" => Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.min(1)
      })
end

defmodule SignalExamples.AreaCompleted do
  use Jido.Signal,
    type: "example.area.completed",
    dataschema: "urn:example:area:completed:1",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{"area" => Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.min(1)})
end

defmodule SignalExamples.LabelRequested do
  use Jido.Signal,
    type: "example.label.requested",
    dataschema: "urn:example:label:requested:1",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{"text" => Zoi.string()})
end

defmodule SignalExamples.AreaFailed do
  use Jido.Signal,
    type: "example.area.failed",
    dataschema: "urn:example:area:failed:1",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{"code" => Zoi.literal("area_limit")})
end
```

## Validate The Complete Signal

The named Zoi transform below passes a native Signal to a one-definition
Catalog. Catalog checks the envelope, exact type, exact `dataschema`, extension
context, and data. It returns the validated Signal, including any data defaults
or transforms. It does not construct a new Signal or replace its identity.

The Catalog is a local value. It performs no registration or dispatch. A
definition without a `dataschema` accepts only Signals without a `dataschema`.

```elixir
defmodule SignalExamples.Boundary do
  def validate(signal, definition, _opts) do
    catalog = Jido.Signal.Catalog.new!([definition])

    case Jido.Signal.Catalog.validate(catalog, signal) do
      {:ok, validated} -> {:ok, validated}
      {:error, error} -> {:error, "Signal validation failed: #{error.details.code}"}
    end
  end

  def child_options(parent) do
    extensions =
      parent.extensions
      |> Map.take(["traceparent", "tracestate"])
      |> Map.put("causationid", parent.id)

    [source: "/examples/area", subject: parent.subject, extensions: extensions]
  end
end

defmodule SignalExamples.CalculateArea do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "example_calculate_area",
    schema:
      Zoi.object(%{
        signal:
          Zoi.any()
          |> Zoi.transform({SignalExamples.Boundary, :validate, [SignalExamples.AreaRequested]})
      }),
    output_schema:
      Zoi.object(%{
        signal:
          Zoi.any()
          |> Zoi.transform({SignalExamples.Boundary, :validate, [SignalExamples.AreaCompleted]})
      })

  @impl true
  def run(%{signal: request}, _context) do
    area = request.data["width"] * request.data["height"]

    if area > 10_000 do
      {:error,
       Jido.Action.Error.execution_error("Area exceeds the example limit", %{
         code: :area_limit,
         retry: false
       })}
    else
      completion =
        SignalExamples.AreaCompleted.new!(
          %{"area" => area},
          SignalExamples.Boundary.child_options(request)
        )

      {:ok, %{signal: completion}}
    end
  end
end

defmodule SignalExamples.PrepareLabel do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "example_prepare_label",
    schema:
      Zoi.object(%{
        signal:
          Zoi.any()
          |> Zoi.transform({SignalExamples.Boundary, :validate, [SignalExamples.AreaCompleted]})
      }),
    output_schema:
      Zoi.object(%{
        signal:
          Zoi.any()
          |> Zoi.transform({SignalExamples.Boundary, :validate, [SignalExamples.LabelRequested]})
      })

  @impl true
  def run(%{signal: completion}, _context) do
    request =
      SignalExamples.LabelRequested.new!(
        %{"text" => "Area: #{completion.data["area"]}"},
        SignalExamples.Boundary.child_options(completion)
      )

    {:ok, %{signal: request}}
  end
end
```

Keep the Signal in a named map field. Action input and normal success output
are maps; the nested `signal` value remains a native `%Jido.Signal{}`. Use
`Jido.Exec.run/4` for input validation, callback execution, and output validation.
A direct `run/2` callback call skips these validation steps.

## Construct A Request From The Previous Result

The result reference creates the dependency. The Flow does not prebuild both
requests. `PrepareLabel` constructs the label request from the validated area
completion. Returning that request does not print a label or dispatch a Signal.
The caller decides what to do with it.

```elixir
defmodule SignalExamples.AreaLabelFlow do
  use Jido.Flow, name: "example_area_label"

  flow do
    step "area", action: SignalExamples.CalculateArea, params: %{signal: input(:signal)}
    step "label", action: SignalExamples.PrepareLabel, params: %{signal: result("area", :signal)}

    output result("label")
  end
end

request =
  SignalExamples.AreaRequested.new!(%{"width" => 3, "height" => 4},
    id: "area-request-42",
    source: "/examples/caller",
    subject: "rectangle-42",
    time: "2026-01-01T00:00:00Z",
    extensions: %{
      "traceparent" => "00-4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-00f067aa0ba902b7-01",
      "causationid" => "caller-request-7"
    }
  )

{:ok, %{signal: label_request}} = Jido.Exec.run(SignalExamples.AreaLabelFlow, %{signal: request})
%{"text" => "Area: 12"} = label_request.data
```

## Identity, Trace, And Causation

Validation preserves the supplied ID, time, source, subject, and valid extension
context. It can normalize data as the definition requires. Do not reconstruct
an incoming Signal just to validate it.

Each newly constructed completion or request has a new ID. In this example,
`causationid` is an application-owned extension that identifies the immediate
parent Signal. The completion points to the area request. The label request
points to the completion. The example copies only trace context and subject;
it does not copy all request extensions or reuse the parent's event time.

The caller owns incoming trace context. The example retains `traceparent` and
`tracestate` without creating a span. Neither Exec nor Catalog creates a Signal
trace or causation link automatically. If an application creates child spans,
its tracing layer must supply the corresponding context. Source, event time,
trace policy, and causation policy belong to the application.

## Failure Signals Belong To The Consumer

Wrong type, `dataschema`, or data fails input validation before Action work.
An invalid completion fails output validation. Exec returns an Action error;
it does not convert that error into a domain Signal or an effect request.

An application can map a known domain failure to its own failure definition.
It must retain the original request for identity and causation. Do not publish
the full exception or private input as failure data. Unexpected validation or
execution failures need a separate application policy.

```elixir
large_request =
  SignalExamples.AreaRequested.new!(%{"width" => 101, "height" => 100},
    source: "/examples/caller"
  )

{:error, %Jido.Action.Error.ExecutionFailureError{details: %{code: :area_limit}}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(SignalExamples.CalculateArea, %{signal: large_request})

failure =
  SignalExamples.AreaFailed.new!(
    %{"code" => "area_limit"},
    SignalExamples.Boundary.child_options(large_request)
  )

"area_limit" = failure.data["code"]
```

The consumer constructs `failure` only after it matches the known error code.
It owns storage and delivery. A failed Flow has no executable effect batch,
and `PrepareLabel` does not run after the area Action fails.

## Schema Export Limit

The Catalog transform checks the complete Signal at runtime. It starts from
`Zoi.any()`, so Action JSON Schema export cannot describe the Signal envelope,
fixed definition identity, or nested data structure at this field. Named Zoi
callbacks are executable checks, not JSON Schema constraints.

```elixir
exported = SignalExamples.CalculateArea.to_json()
true = exported["input_schema"]["properties"]["signal"] == %{}
true = exported["output_schema"]["properties"]["signal"] == %{}

data_schema = Zoi.to_json_schema(SignalExamples.AreaRequested.schema())
```

`data_schema` describes the declared request data separately. It is not a schema
for a complete Signal and must not be used as one. The native struct also differs
from the CloudEvents wire map: wire extensions are flat attributes. Use Signal
serialization APIs at that boundary rather than treating `Map.from_struct/1`
or the Action schema export as a wire codec.

Complete Signal structure in exported Action schemas remains dependent on
[Signal #222](https://github.com/agentjido/jido_signal/issues/222).
That helper must preserve Catalog validation and normalization while exposing
the envelope and definition data to JSON Schema. Custom transforms and
refinements can still require runtime checks.

## Check The Boundaries

The [example tests](https://github.com/agentjido/jido/blob/release/v3/test/examples/guides/typed_signals_test.exs)
run this notebook against Jido's Action and Signal dependencies. They check
input, output, Flow, identity, causation, and schema export.

Run them from the Jido package:

```text
mix test test/examples/guides/typed_signals_test.exs --include example
```
